From: RenoDX AMD bridge experiment
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] dlss: scale Streamline motion vectors in their resource domain

Streamline defines mvecScale as the factor used to normalize the game's raw
motion vectors. FidelityFX expects the scale that converts the sampled motion
vectors into screen-space pixel displacement.

When a title provides display-resolution motion vectors, multiplying by render
resolution is wrong. Use the actual tagged motion-vector resource dimensions;
this also matches the established Streamline translation used by OptiScaler.

--- a/src/utils/dlss/ffx_upscale_backend_dx12.hpp
+++ b/src/utils/dlss/ffx_upscale_backend_dx12.hpp
@@
   const auto& resources = state.resources;
   const auto& constants = *state.constants;
   const auto render_size = ResourceDimensions(resources.scaling_input);
   const auto output_size = ResourceDimensions(resources.scaling_output);
+  const auto motion_vector_size = ResourceDimensions(resources.motion_vectors);
+
+  if (!motion_vector_size.IsValid()) {
+    return sl::Result::eErrorMissingInputParameter;
+  }
@@
-  // Streamline mvecScale normalizes the game's raw vectors to [-1,1]. FSR
-  // expects screen-space pixel vectors, so apply the render dimensions after
-  // the Streamline normalization scale.
+  // Streamline mvecScale normalizes the game's raw vectors. FidelityFX expects
+  // the scale that converts those sampled values into screen-space pixels.
+  // Use the actual MV resource domain: render-resolution MVs use render size,
+  // while display-resolution MVs use presentation size.
   dispatch.motionVectorScale = {
-      constants.mvecScale.x * static_cast<float>(render_size.width),
-      constants.mvecScale.y * static_cast<float>(render_size.height)};
+      constants.mvecScale.x * static_cast<float>(motion_vector_size.width),
+      constants.mvecScale.y * static_cast<float>(motion_vector_size.height)};

